+- New `tests/dictionary-smoke.cjs` (`npm run test:dictionary`), in the real app with two documents open. 5 checks: an empty dictionary; Add to Dictionary from the right-click menu clears the red lines in both documents, and Ignore All adds nothing to the dictionary; Clear asks, lists the words, and Cancel changes nothing; Clear removes the words and they are marked again in both documents, about 1.2 s after leaving Settings, while the ignored word stays ignored; one word added and cleared again.
+- Each safeguard was taken out in turn in a copy of the source, and the tests failed where expected: the caret handling (Ignore All check 3), the delayed recheck (check 6), the recheck after Ignore All (check 1), waiting for the caret (check 9), the recheck after Clear (dictionary check 4), waiting for a hidden editor (dictionary check 5).
+- `tests/desktop-smoke.cjs`: 19 checks; new are the Help menu in the menu row and by Alt+H, and the About box's text, version, buttons and the two addresses it opens.
+- `tests/spelling-smoke.cjs` (7 checks: opened, pasted, typed and source-edited text), `settings-smoke.cjs`, `word-count-smoke.cjs`, `typography-smoke.cjs`, `startup-smoke.cjs`, `close-restore-smoke.cjs`, `close-command-smoke.cjs`, `npm run test:equivalence`, `tests/theme-browser-smoke.py` and `tests/colors-browser-smoke.py`: passed, unchanged.
+- The Ignore All and dictionary tests were each run several times in a row without a failure after the last change. Before it, the dictionary test failed once in three runs; that failure is what led to the fourth change above.
+- First-letter wrappers and letter spacing: in a line with 23 wrappers the text ends 0.36 px further right than without them (at most 1/64 px per wrapper); line breaks and paragraph heights are the same, and kerning across the wrapper is kept.
+- The packaged `resources/app` from the Windows ZIP passed the Ignore All, dictionary, spelling, settings and desktop checks.
+
+**Speed.** Key press to painted frame, medians, same machine and method as for 1.3.0.
+
+- *Nothing ignored.* In a 2 MB document (248,000 words, 4,400 paragraphs) typing takes 31–39 ms in 1.3.1 and 35–36 ms in 1.3.0; going to the Markdown source view takes 0.7 s and coming back 1.3–1.5 s in both. The Ignore All code does no work while nothing is ignored.
+- *An ordinary document* (100 KB, 12,500 words) with a word ignored that occurs 207 times: typing 7 ms before and 7–8 ms after. Ignore All is drawn in under 0.1 s, and the other red lines are back 1.2 s after it.
+- *The 2 MB document with a word ignored that occurs 4,950 times*, in 3,957 of its paragraphs: Ignore All is drawn after 0.9–1.3 s; the other red lines are gone for 3.1–3.6 s (the whole document is rechecked 4.3–4.5 s after the click, as after a paste). Typing then takes 61–66 ms instead of 31–37 ms. A profile shows where the extra time goes, per key press: 17 ms in Chromium, which walks the whole document's text after every key press and finds an extra element and two extra text nodes for every wrapper; 9 ms in ProseMirror, which looks up the decorations of every paragraph with a scan that gets longer the more paragraphs have any; 2 ms in React's reading of the caret. Returning from the source view takes 1.7–1.9 s.
+- *The same document with a word that occurs 24,751 times* (every tenth word): Ignore All is drawn after 1.7 s, the recheck is done 10 s after the click, and typing takes 81–84 ms.
+- So the cost is not measurable for words that occur up to some hundreds of times, and grows with the number of occurrences. A word that occurs thousands of times in a book-length document belongs in the dictionary.
+
+**Windows portable distribution.** Assembled by `scripts/repack-windows-from-1.3.0.py` from the 1.3.0 portable release. Its Electron 44.6.0 runtime is unchanged. In `resources/app`, `package.json`, `LICENSE`, `dist/index.html`, the JavaScript bundle and the four `desktop` files named above differ from 1.3.0; `START-HERE.txt`, `KEYBOARD-SHORTCUTS.md`, `APP-LICENSE.txt` and `RELEASE-VALIDATION.txt` are this release's. In `PicoMarQ.exe` only the version information changes from 1.3.0 to 1.3.1 (two strings and two numbers of the fixed version record), as checked independently with `pefile`: 4 bytes of `.rsrc` differ, every other section is identical, and the entry point is the same.
+Baseline (1.3.0) executable SHA-256: `33497603f3fa99975cfca18df33b3f6856042f2a2107200d3bcdaabdc374dcec`
+1.3.1 executable SHA-256: `9e0b2b9a293457b1441c819a69e57e65a8454267080655975f70fede2991845c`
 
 **Limitations.**
 
-- Not run on Windows. Nothing in this release depends on the operating system, but a check on Windows 10/11 is still worth doing.
-- The size in Settings is the font size in CSS pixels, as before; only its label changed. At 100% text zoom, 15 looks about like 11-point type in a word processor.
-- In source view a selection that cuts through Markdown syntax is counted as the text it would be on its own. Selecting only a link's address, or only the description inside an image, counts those words, although the document count leaves them out. The same can happen with single lines taken out of a table, a code block or another construct of several lines. In the sample `docs/test-documents/PicoMarQ-Torture-Test.md` (916 words) the blocks selected one at a time add up to exactly 916, the 144 lines selected one at a time to 924, and none of 3,000 random selections counted more words than a plain count of the selected characters.
-- Hyphenated words count as two words, in the selection as in the total (unchanged).
-- Found while writing the test, the same in 1.2.6 and 1.2.7, and not changed: in the first 0.15 s or so after leaving source view, the editor places the caret, and an arrow key pressed in that instant right after Ctrl+A can be ignored. Only a script is that fast; the status bar shows the selection the editor really has.
+- Not run on Windows. Ignore All and the rechecks are in Chromium's page engine and in the editor, the same on every system. Three things are Windows' own and could only be read from Electron's source, not tried: the native boxes for About and for the question before clearing; opening the two addresses in the default browser; and the Windows spelling dictionary. On Windows, Electron passes Add to Dictionary on to Windows' own user dictionary as well as to PicoMarQ's list, and passes each removal on in the same way. Clear Custom Dictionary removes the words in PicoMarQ's list from both; words that are only in Windows' dictionary (added from another program) are not listed and not touched.
+- Ignore All exists only where spelling is checked, in the formatted view. The list is not saved: after PicoMarQ restarts and restores its documents, the words are marked again.
+- During a recheck every red line is gone for about a second, and for several seconds in a very long document. Besides a paste (since 1.2.7) this now happens after Ignore All, a second after an ignored word is typed in a paragraph of more than 900 characters (about 150 words), after Undo or Replace changes text at an ignored word, and on returning to a document after Clear Custom Dictionary.
+- With thousands of ignored occurrences in a very long document typing gets slower; see Speed.
+- If Settings is opened within about a second of choosing Ignore All (longer in a very long document, while it is still being rechecked), Chromium abandons the recheck: red lines far from the ignored word stay away until the caret next moves in the document, and return a second after that. Opening the Markdown source view instead does no harm, and neither did Settings after a paste, in 1.3.0 or 1.3.1.
+- Found while testing, the same in 1.3.0, and not changed: Find and Replace are hard on the red lines. While Find shows its matches, the paragraphs that contain a match lose their red lines, and these do not come back when Find is closed; text put in by Replace is not checked. Both last until the document is rechecked: by a paste, by Ignore All, or by closing and opening the document. A recheck when Find closes would cure it.
+- The limitations of 1.3.0 stand: the size in Settings is in CSS pixels; a selection that cuts through Markdown syntax in source view is counted as the text it would be on its own; hyphenated words count as two.
